      "writtenOn": "2023-03-17T17:47:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s failing on cuttlefish. But it would fail with Trusty too.\n\nThe HAL spec doesn\u0027t require that challenges outside the range must fail. The challenge comes from the server, and it\u0027s basically treated as opaque by device code. It makes sense that we check that the HAL supports the entire size range. But I think the onus of ensuring that challenge is within specified range should be on the server. \n\nAdding the size check in the HAL will also make it difficult to expand the range, like we did earlier.",
      "parentUuid": "315f4aea_13abf263",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 756,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 756,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "7887285d8a99280d7c7365be2f1715d358193f35",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4e6b4e6b_e3a7f71a",
        "filename": "security/rkp/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 756,
      "author": {
        "id": 1833664
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-17T17:48:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Fair points! I agree, there\u0027s nothing wrong with a HAL being \"too accepting\".\n\nThough, realistically, I would expect an SE to have strict limits due to memory concerns. So we should probably remove this test.",
      "parentUuid": "7810fa0f_2a9001ab",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 756,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 756,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "7887285d8a99280d7c7365be2f1715d358193f35",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0fd9da4b_f8aacb65",
        "filename": "security/rkp/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 756,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115498
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-20T03:00:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In that case, I\u0027m more in favor of just going to a challenge size of `0..64`, enforce that `challenge \u003e 64` fails, and leave it there. I don\u0027t think we\u0027d have a need for a challenge greater than 64 bytes, and even if we did we wouldn\u0027t have a guarantee we could migrate to it due to all of the implementations in the ecosystem that may actually expect an upperbounded challenge size due to the spec here.\n\nLowerbounding at 16 bytes seems like an arbitrary stopping point if we\u0027re cool with HALs accepting basically empty challenges and leaving it up to the server to configure the challenge.",
      "parentUuid": "4e6b4e6b_e3a7f71a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 756,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 756,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "7887285d8a99280d7c7365be2f1715d358193f35",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ed0ea00a_f79b83ea",
        "filename": "security/rkp/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 756,
      "author": {
        "id": 1833664
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-20T17:40:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think the bounds enforcement in VTS provides any value.\n\nIf an implementation wants to accept arbitrarily-sized challenges, so be it. Our infrastructure requires 16..64 support, and that\u0027s it. It seems inconsequential if an implementation supports other sizes for challenge.\n\nWhat bugs/interop problems does this test prevent? I cannot think of any. At best it\u0027s a nanny for implementations that can be attacked with very large buffers.",
      "parentUuid": "0fd9da4b_f8aacb65",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 756,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 756,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "7887285d8a99280d7c7365be2f1715d358193f35",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d5632a2d_757c3e2e",
        "filename": "security/rkp/aidl/vts/functional/VtsRemotelyProvisionedComponentTests.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 756,
      "author": {
        "id": 1115498
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-21T00:13:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "A worst case scenario (judge for yourself how likely it is) - We could always accidentally check in a server change in such a way that we start sending some order of magnitude larger challenge, which if the HAL does not explicitly reject may cause the TEE to blow up and reboot the device.\n\nAnother scenario - the HAL author doesn\u0027t think to sanitize the challenge length, leading to some buffer overflow vulnerability when users maliciously pass in challenges greater than size 64.\n\nI think it\u0027s better to exactly validate specs where possible to turn low-hanging unknowns into tested knowns, especially when they\u0027ll be implemented by a very broad ecosystem. We can\u0027t proactively think ahead and fix every problem, but this seems like a reasonable check.",
      "parentUuid": "ed0ea00a_f79b83ea",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 756,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 756,
        "endChar": 20
